WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
(suspenseful music)
(water gurgling)

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.820
- [Narrator] During the Cretaceous period,

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.690
Mosasaurs were among
the oceans most fearsome

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.663
and successful predators.

00:00:13.476 --> 00:00:16.300
Mosasaurs were marine reptiles
that are thought to be

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.700
closely related to snakes
and monitor lizards.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.110
They were highly adaptable

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:22.950
and many different species evolved

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.130
and came to dominate
ocean habitats worldwide.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.183
Some even took to fresh
water rivers to hunt.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.810
Many prowled the open ocean,
devouring fish, sharks,

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.721
plesiosaurs, sea turtles, sea birds,

00:00:36.721 --> 00:00:40.193
and sometimes even smaller mosasaurs.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.370
Mosasaur size varied greatly.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.750
Two of the largest mosasaurs
were Mosasaurus and Tylosaurus.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.720
Each stretched 30 to 50
feet, longer than a T-Rex.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.160
Meanwhile, many smaller mosasaurs

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.440
were no larger than a dolphin.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.940
Yet most species, no
matter how large or small,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.610
could be characterized
by a long serpentine body

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.980
with a powerful tail which
they moved side to side

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.780
as they slithered through the water.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.940
They had paddles that were
likely used for stability

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.240
and large heads that had
powerful, flexible jaws.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.290
Their jaws contained two
rows of conical teeth,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.723
designed to chomp and hold prey
before swallowing it whole.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.450
The top marine reptiles since the Triassic

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.070
had been ichthyosaurs and plesiosaurs,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.230
but they were in decline

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.250
when mosasaurs came onto the
scene during the Cretaceous,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.833
leaving room for a new apex predator.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.530
According to theory, mosasaurs evolved

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.350
from terrestrial lizards
that adapted to the oceans

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.660
by the middle of the Cretaceous.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.380
Then, during the 20 to 30 million years

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.150
leading up to the
extinction of the dinosaurs,

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.293
mosasaurs rapidly adapted to
ocean habitats the world over.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.600
We know this because
fossils have been found

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.553
on every continent, including Antarctica.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.680
When dinosaurs became extinct

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.520
some 65 and a half million years ago,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.313
mosasaurs vanished from
the fossil record as well.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.700
While it's not known

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.850
whether they died off
instantly or gradually,

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.920
one thing is for certain:

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.160
the ocean has never again
seen marine reptiles

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.285
as massive and as great as mosasaurs.

00:02:30.285 --> 00:02:33.702
(gentle cinematic music)

